e7\n- G: 256^2 * 1280 = 8.4e7\n- p@G: 2 * 20 * 256^3 = 6.7e8\n- p@v: 20 * 256^2 * 64 = 8.4e7\n\nSo QKV is ~46% of flops, p@G is ~25%, and the rest is ~29%.\n\nFor case 4 (B=1, L=256, C=640, H=10, D=64):\n- QKV: 3 * 256 * 640 * 10 * 64 = 3.1e8\n- qk^T: 10 * 256^2 * 64 = 4.2e7\n- G: 256^2 * 640 = 4.2e7\n- p@G: 2 * 10 * 256^3 = 3.4e8\n- p@v: 10 * 256^2 * 64 = 4.2e7\n\nSo QKV ~42%, p@G ~45%, rest ~13%. Here p@G dominates!\n\nThe biggest opportunity for case 4 (and other L-dominated cases) is to reduce the p@G cost.\n\nCan we reduce p@G cost? We need two p@G operations: p1@G and p_mix@G.\n\nWait - do we need both? Let me re-examine. We need:\n- ||u1|| = norm of p1 @ x → uses p1@G\n- ||m|| = norm of p_mix @ x → uses p_mix@G\n\nSince p_mix = (1-β)p0 + β p1, we have:\n- p_mix @ G = (1-β)(p0@G) + β(p1@G)\n\nSo if we compute p0@G and p1@G, we can get p_mix@G by a linear combination. But that's still 2 p@G operations. Same count.\n\nWhat about computing p0@G differently? p0 = softmax(A). Not sure.\n\n**Big idea: Can we compute ||m|| directly from m = p_mix @ x without using the Gram trick?**\n\nm = p_mix @ x has shape (B, H, L, C). Computing m requires B*H*L^2*C flops. For case 3: 20 * 256^2 * 1280 = 1.68e9 flops. That's MORE than 2 * p@G (6.7e8). So Gram trick is better.\n\nBut for case 4 (C=640): m would be 10 * 256^2 * 640 = 4.2e8 flops. And 2 * p@G = 2 * 10 * 256^3 = 3.4e8. p@G is still cheaper.\n\nActually wait: the break-even is when C = L. When C > L, p@G (which is L^3 per head) is cheaper than p@x (which is L^2*C per head). When C < L, p@x is cheaper.\n\nFor case 4: C=640, L=256. C > L, so p@G is cheaper. Good.\n\nFor ALL cases actually: C >= 512, L <= 256. So C > L always, and the Gram trick is beneficial.\n\nOK so the Gram trick is the right approach.\n\nNow, can we make the 2 p@G operations faster? They're L^3 each. For L=256, that's 16M per head per matmul.\n\nOn a single CPU core at ~3 GFLOPS: 2 * H * L^3 / 3e9 = 2*20*16.7M/3G = 223ms for 20 heads. But we measured