64, C=512, H=8, D=64 → HD=512), the cat creates a 1536 × 512 = 786K element tensor = 3MB. That's not huge. And we save 2 kernel launches (from 3 matmuls to 1). \n\nLet me test this properly.\n\nActually, wait. Let me look at this from the perspective of the evaluation metric. The metric is the mean of per-case candidate/control ratios, mean across seeds. So all cases matter equally. Cases 0, 1, 2, 5 are relatively small and might benefit more from reducing overhead. Cases 3, 4 are larger and benefit more from compute optimization.\n\nThe current solution achieves ~0.46 ratio overall. The worst cases (highest ratio) are case 4 (~0.7-0.99) and case 3 (~0.49-0.88). Let me focus on making those faster.\n\nFor case 4 (L=256, C=640, H=10, D=64), p@G dominates. Two p@G operations: 2 * 10 * 256^3 = 335M ops = ~6.7ms measured. QKV: 3 * 256*640*10*64 = 315M ops = ?\n\nWait let me measure case 4 breakdown properly.\nThe CPU achieves ~40-50 GFLOPS for p@G. Let me now look at where the biggest remaining bottleneck is across all cases and try a meaningful optimization.\n\nLet me profile case 4 (the worst case, ratio ~0.7-0.99) in detail.\n<br>\n\nActually, let me try a completely different approach. What if we compute the norm differently? \n\nCurrently:\n- p1G = p1 @ G (BHL^3)\n- norm1_sq = (p1G * p1).sum(-1)\n- pmG = p_mix @ G (BHL^3)  \n- normm_sq = (pmG * p_mix).sum(-1)\n\nAlternative: compute `u1 = p1 @ x` then `norm1 = u1.norm(dim=-1)`. This is B*H*L^2*C flops instead of B*H*L^3.\nFor case 4 (L=256, C=640, H=10): BHL^2C = 10*256^2*640 = 419M vs BHL^3 = 10*256^3 = 167M.\nGram trick is better (167M vs 419M).\n\nFor normm: same thing, Gram trick is better.\n\nOK, Gram trick is right.\n\nWhat about: can we compute both norms with a single p@G call?\nIf we stack p1 and p_mix along the batch dimension, we get (2*B*H, L, L) @ (B*H, L, L)... wait no, G is (B, L, L) not (B*H, L, L).\n\nActually, G is the same for all heads (since G = x@x^T and x is shared across heads). So when we do `p1 @ G_exp` where `G_exp = G.unsqueeze(1)` with shape (B, 1, L, L), PyTorch broadcasts this to (B, H, L, L).\n\nIf we stack p1 and p_mix as (B, 2H, L, L), and expand G to (B, 2H, L, L), then one matmul computes both. That's 1 matmul of size (B, 2H, L, L) @ (B, 2H, L, L) ... wait no. \n\nActually, it's (B, 2H, L, L) @ (B, 1, L, L) → (B, 2H, L, L) via broadcasting. This is the same total work as 2 separate (B, H, L, L) @ (B, 1, L, L) matmuls.\n\nThe total flops are the same: 2 * B * H * L^3. One big matmul vs two smaller ones. Might be slightly faster due to reduced kernel launch overhead.\n\nLet me try this approach.\n\nLet me try a focused optimization: stack p1 and p_mix for a single combined p@G matmul, and try to reduce overhead in other ways. Let me implement and test this.\n</think>\n\nLet me try an approach where we stack both attention matrices for a single p@G matmul, and also try to squeeze out more performance.\n<br>\n\nActually, let me first understand the overall bottleneck better. Let me write a clean version with a few targeted optimizations and test it properly.\n\nKey optimizations to try:\n1. Stack p1 and p_mix into one batch for a single combined `p@G` matmul (reduces kernel launch overhead)\n2. Compute `p_mix @ v` as part of the same stacking strategy\n3. Use `torch.bmm` with explicit 3D views where it helps\n\nLet me implement and test.\n</think>\n\nLet me implement a version with stacked p@G computation and other micro-optimizations, then run the full evaluation.\n\nFirst, let me quickly verify the correctness and speed of the stacked approach:\n</think>\n</tool_calls>\n<br>\n\nLet me try a more creative optimization. Instead of computing p0 and p1 separately and then mixing, what if we restructure the computation to minimize the number of large matmuls?\n\nActually, let me try the simplest meaningful improvement first and run the full evaluation: compute G immediately (it doesn't depend on q,k,v), and use a stacked p@G approach. Let me implement it properly.\n<br>\n</tool_calls>\n\nLet me take a step back and think about the most impactful optimization. The current 0.461 ratio is already very good. Let me try one meaningful improvement: **fuse the two p@G operations by stacking**, and also **compute qk^T via a different order to avoid the permute**. Let me implement and run the full evaluation.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python << 'PYEOF'\nimport torch, time, math\nimport sys\n\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\ntorch.set_num_interop_threads(1)\n\n# Current best\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef attention_current(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta):\n    B, L, C = x.shape\n    H, D = wq.shape[0], wq.shape[1]\n    inv_sqrt_d = 1.0 / math.sqrt(D)\n    HD = H * D\n    BHL = B * H * L\n    xr = x.reshape(B * L, C)\n\n    q = (xr @ wq.reshape(HD, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous()\n    k = (xr @ wk.reshape(HD, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous()\n    v = (xr @ wv.reshape(HD, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous()\n\n    logits = q @ k.transpose(-1, -2)\n    logits.mul_(inv_sqrt_d)\n\n    p0 = logits.view(BHL, L).softmax(dim=-1).view(B, H, L, L)\n\n    if alpha == 1.0:\n        y = p0 @ v\n    else:\n        a_p = 0.5 * (1.0 + alpha)\n        a_m = 0.5 * (1.0 - alpha)\n        p1_logits = logits.mul(a_p)\n        p1_logits.add_(logits.transpose(-1, -2), alpha=a_m)\n        p1 = p1_logits.view(BHL, L).softmax(dim=-1).view(B, H, L, L)\n\n        p_mix = torch.lerp(p0, p1, beta)\n\n        G = x @ x.transpose(-1, -2)\n        G_exp = G.unsqueeze(1)\n\n        p1G = p1 @ G_exp\n        norm1_sq = p1G.mul_(p1).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n        pmG = p_mix @ G_exp\n        normm_sq = pmG.mul_(p_mix).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n        norm1 = norm1_sq.sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n        normm = normm_sq.sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n        ratio = norm1.div_(normm).clamp_(0.25, 4.0)\n\n        y = (p_mix @ v).mul_(ratio)\n\n    return y.transpose(1, 2).flatten(2).contiguous()\n\n# New version: stack p1 and p_mix for single p@G matmul\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef attention_stacked(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta):\n    B, L, C = x.shape\n    H, D = wq.shape[0], wq.shape[1]\n    inv_sqrt_d = 1.0 / math.sqrt(D)\n    HD = H * D\n    BHL = B * H * L\n    BH = B * H\n    xr = x.reshape(B * L, C)\n\n    q = (xr @ wq.reshape(HD, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous()\n    k = (xr @ wk.reshape(HD, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous()\n    v = (xr @ wv.reshape(HD, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous()\n\n    logits = q @ k.transpose(-1, -2)\n    logits.mul_(inv_sqrt_d)\n\n    p0 = logits.view(BHL, L).softmax(dim=-1).view(B, H, L, L)\n\n    if alpha == 1.0:\n        y = p0 @ v\n    else:\n        a_p = 0.5 * (1.0 + alpha)\n        a_m = 0.5 * (1.0 - alpha)\n        p1_logits = logits.mul(a_p)\n        p1_logits.add_(logits.transpose(-1, -2), alpha=a_m)\n        p1 = p1_logits.view(BHL, L).softmax(dim=-1).view(B, H, L, L)\n\n        # Compute G early\n        G = x @ x.transpose(-1, -2)\n        \n        # Stack p1 and p0 along head dim for combined p@G\n        # Shape: (B, 2H, L, L)\n        p_stack = torch.cat([p1, p0], dim=1)\n        # Expand G: (B, 2H, L, L)\n        G_stack = G.unsqueeze(1).expand(B, 2 * H, L, L)\n        \n        # One big matmul: (B, 2H, L, L) @ (B, 2H, L, L) -> (B, 2H, L, L)\n        pG_stack = p_stack @ G_stack\n        \n        p1G = pG_stack[:, :H, :, :]\n        p0G = pG_stack[:, H:, :, :]\n        \n        # norm1 = sqrt((p1G * p1).sum(-1))\n        norm1_sq = p1G.mul(p1).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n        norm1 = norm1_sq.sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n        \n        # normm: m = (1-beta)*u0 + beta*u1 = p_mix @ x\n        # ||m||\"'^2 = ((1-beta)*p0 + beta*p1) @ G * ((1-beta)*p0 + beta*p1) summed\n        # = (1-b)''^2 (p0G*p0).sum + b''^2 (p1G*p1).sum + 2*(1-b)*b * (p0G*p1).sum\n        # Note: (p0G * p1).sum = (p1G * p0).sum since G is symmetric\n        one_minus_b = 1.0 - beta\n        cross = p0G.mul(p1).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n        norm0_sq = p0G.mul(p0).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n        normm_sq = one_minus_b * one_minus_b * norm0_sq + beta * beta * norm1_sq + 2.0 * one_minus_b * beta * cross\n        normm = normm_sq.sqrt_().clamp_min_(1e-6)\n        \n        ratio = norm1.div_(normm).clamp_(0.25, 4.0)\n        \n        # Output: p_mix @ v = (1-beta)*(p0@v) + beta*(p1@v)\n        y0 = p0 @ v\n        y1 = p1 @ v\n        y = y0.lerp_(y1, beta).mul_(ratio)\n\n    return y.transpose(1, 2).flatten(2).contiguous()\n\n# Test correctness\nimport importlib.util\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"starter\", \"/workspace/environment/starter/method.py\")\nstarter = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\nspec.loader.exec_module(starter)\n\ncases = [\n    (1, 64, 512, 8, 64, 1.05, 5.0),\n    (1, 128, 640, 10, 64, 1.1, 4.0),\n    (1, 128, 1280, 20, 64, 1.15, 3.0),\n    (1, 256, 1280, 20, 64, 1.05, 7.5),\n    (1, 256, 640, 10, 64, 1.2, 5.0),\n    (2, 128, 512, 8, 64, 1.0, 5.0),\n]\n\nall_ok = True\nfor i, (B, L, C, H, D, alpha, beta) in enumerate(cases):\n    torch.manual_seed(42 + i)\n    x = torch.randn(B, L, C)\n    wq = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    wk = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    wv = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    \n    y_ref = starter.attention(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n    y_new = attention_stacked(x.clone(), wq.clone(), wk.clone(), wv.clone(), alpha, beta)\n    \n    err = (y_new - y_ref).abs().max()\n    tol = 3e-5 + 3e-4 * y_ref.abs()\n    max_viol = ((y_new - y_ref).abs() - tol).max()\n    ok = max_viol <= 0\n    all_ok = all_ok and ok\n    print(f'\"'Case {i}: max_err={err:.2e}, max_viol={max_viol:.2e}, ok={ok}')\n\nprint(f'All pass: {all_ok}')\n\n# Speed comparison\nprint('\\\\n--- Speed comparison (median of 11) ---')\nfor i, (B, L, C, H, D, alpha, beta) in enumerate(cases):\n    torch.manual_seed(42 + i)\n    x = torch.randn(B, L, C)\n    wq = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    wk = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    wv = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n    \n    for _ in range(3):\n        attention_current(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n        attention_stacked(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n    \n    n = 11\n    curr_times = []\n    new_times = []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n        attention_current(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n        curr_times.append((time.process_time_ns() - t0) / 1e6)\n        \n        t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n        attention_stacked(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta)\n        new_times.append((time.process_time_ns() - t0) / 1e6)\n    \n    curr_times.sort()\n    new_times.sort()\n    print(f'Case {i}: curr={curr_times[n//2]:.2f}ms new={new_times[n//2]:.2f}ms ratio={new_times[n//2]/curr_times[n//2]:.3f}')\nPYEOF\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
