{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_88","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python << 'PYEOF'\nimport torch, time, math\n\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\ntorch.set_num_interop_threads(1)\n\n# Let me try a more aggressive optimization: \n# Compute q and k in (B, L, H, D) layout and use einsum for logits? \n# No, einsum is slower.\n\n# What if we compute the logits in B, L, H, L format?\n# logits[b, l, h, l'] = sum_d q[b, l, h, d] * k[b, l', h, d]\n# This is: q is (B, L, H, D), k is (B, L, H, D)\n# Result: (B, L, H, L) or (B, H, L, L)\n\n# Actually let me try computing logits without permuting q and k.\n# If q and k are (B, L, H, D), can we compute logits efficiently?\n# logits[b,h,i,j] = sum_d q[b,i,h,d] * k[b,j,h,d]\n\n# Using einsum: \\\"bihd,bjhd->bhij\\\"\n# But einsum is slow.\n\n# Using matmul: we need (B, H, L, D) @ (B, H, D, L) = (B, H, L, L)\n# So we need to permute anyway.\n\n# What about: compute q as (B*H, L, D) directly?\n# q[b*H+h, l, d] = sum_c x[b,l,c] * w[h,d,c]\n# = x[b,l,:] @ w[h,:,:].T\n# This is like doing H separate matmuls of (L, C) @ (C, D)\n\n# Or: (B*L, C) @ (C, H*D) then reshape to (B, L, H, D) then permute to (B, H, L, D)\n# This is what we already do. The permute is needed.\n\n# Alternative: compute q, k in (BH, L, D) via a different matmul formulation.\n# If we expand x to (BH, L, C) = x.unsqueeze(1).expand(B,H,L,C).reshape(BH, L, C)\n# Then q = x_expanded @ w.reshape(HD, C).T ... no that doesn't work right.\n\n# Actually, let me think about this:\n# w has shape (H, D, C). w.reshape(HD, C) has shape (H*D, C).\n# x has shape (B, L, C). x.reshape(BL, C) has shape (B*L, C).\n# x @ w.T: (BL, C) @ (C, HD) = (BL, HD). \n# q[b*L+l, h*D+d] = sum_c x[b*L+l, c] * w[h*D+d, c] = sum_c x[b,l,c] * w[h,d,c]\n# Correct.\n\n# To get (B, H, L, D): reshape (BL, HD) to (B, L, H, D), then permute to (B, H, L, D).\n# The permute is necessary for the attention matmul pattern.\n\n# The permute+contiguous copies B*L*H*D elements. Tiny cost.\n# The big cost is the 3 projection matmuls and the 2 p@G matmuls.\n\n# Let me try another idea: what if we compute q and k in one matmul \n# by reshaping weights differently?\n# wqk has shape (2, H, D, C). Reshape to (2*H*D, C).\n# qk_flat = xr @ wqk.T  → (BL, 2*HD)\n# q_flat, k_flat = qk_flat.split(HD, dim=1)\n# Same total flops, but one matmul instead of two.\n# We saw that stacked qk is faster for small cases but slower for large cases.\n# But maybe for the specific sizes here it helps overall.\n\n# Actually wait - I showed earlier that stacked is SLOWER for most cases.\n# So that's not the way.\n\n# Let me think about what else can give a big win.\n# The current solution is already 2.3x faster than baseline (0.438 ratio).\n# The baseline uses einsum which is known to be slow.\n\n# What if we reduce the number of softmax calls?\n# We need p0 and p1. Two softmaxes. \n# Can we compute p_mix directly somehow? No, it's defined via p0 and p1.\n\n# What if we compute p1 without computing p0?\n# We still need p_mix which needs both p0 and p1.\n\n# What about the alpha == 1.0 case? That's case 5. Already handled.\n\n# Let me try one more thing: using torch.compile\n# Oh wait, torch.compile is forbidden (jit is in the banned attrs).\n\n# Let me look at the banned attrs again:\n# \\\"jit\\\", \\\"compile\\\" - yes, forbidden.\n\n# What about using torch.nn.functional.linear for the projections?\n# Should be same as matmul.\n\n# OK, let me try a completely different approach: \n# what if we compute p1 @ v at the same time as p_mix @ v by stacking?\n# p_stack = cat([p1, p_mix], dim=1)  → (B, 2H, L, L)\n# v_exp = v.expand(B, 2H, L, D) ... no, v is per-head already.\n# v has shape (B, H, L, D). Each head has its own v.\n# So we can't just stack because p1 and p_mix are different for each head.\n\n# Wait, p1 and p_mix have shape (B, H, L, L) - H heads.\n# If we stack p1 and p_mix as (B, 2H, L, L),\n# and stack v with itself as (B, 2H, L, D),\n# then one big matmul gives (B, 2H, L, D) = p1@v and p_mix@v stacked.\n# Total flops: 2H * L\"'^2 * D (same as two separate H * L''^2 * D).\n# Same total flops, but one big matmul might be more efficient.\n\n# Let me test this!\nB, H, L, D = 1, 20, 256, 64\np = torch.randn(B, H, L, L).softmax(dim=-1)\np2 = torch.randn(B, H, L, L).softmax(dim=-1)\nv = torch.randn(B, H, L, D)\n\n# Separate\ndef separate():\n    y1 = p @ v\n    y2 = p2 @ v\n    return y1, y2\n\n# Stacked\ndef stacked():\n    p_stack = torch.cat([p, p2], dim=1)\n    v_stack = torch.cat([v, v], dim=1)\n    y_stack = p_stack @ v_stack\n    return y_stack[:, :H], y_stack[:, H:]\n\nfor _ in range(5):\n    separate()\n    stacked()\n\nn = 20\ntsep = []\ntstk = []\nfor _ in range(n):\n    t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n    separate()\n    tsep.append((time.process_time_ns() - t0) / 1e6)\n    t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n    stacked()\n    tstk.append((time.process_time_ns() - t0) / 1e6)\n\ntsep.sort()\ntstk.sort()\nprint(f'\"'Separate p@v: {tsep[n//2]:.3f} ms')\nprint(f'Stacked p@v: {tstk[n//2]:.3f} ms')\n\n# But wait, stacking requires cat which takes time. What if we include cat?\ndef stacked_with_cat():\n    ps = torch.cat([p, p2], dim=1)\n    vs = torch.cat([v, v], dim=1)\n    ys = ps @ vs\n    return ys[:, :H], ys[:, H:]\n\ntsc = []\nfor _ in range(n):\n    t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n    stacked_with_cat()\n    tsc.append((time.process_time_ns() - t0) / 1e6)\ntsc.sort()\nprint(f'Stacked (with cat): {tsc[n//2]:.3f} ms')\nPYEOF\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
